  "image_name": "786b9fb80e079732296ff50d2d76bcf0.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two aqueous lithium–air battery concepts and explains why one offers a much higher voltage. In the simpler design, lithium metal on the left is separated from water by a lithium solid-state electrolyte, while a single cathode catalyst on the right handles both charging and discharging in the aqueous side. Lithium ions shuttle through the solid electrolyte and electrons flow through the external circuit.\n\nThe center panel summarizes the electrochemistry: the lithium anode reaction is very negative, and pairing it with oxygen reduction/evolution in water gives an open-circuit voltage of about 3.4 V in alkaline conditions or up to 4.23 V for the acidic oxygen reaction. This highlights the large energy potential of combining Li with aqueous oxygen chemistry.\n\nThe right-hand schematic shows an improved architecture that splits the air electrode into separate OER and ORR catalysts and separates the liquid into anolyte and catholyte regions. A switch-like external circuit routes current differently for charge and discharge, so each catalyst is used for the reaction it is best suited for. Overall, the figure emphasizes interface design, catalyst separation, and the voltage advantage of aqueous Li–air systems.",
